  "conclusions": [
    "The full-UI first long frame is specifically localized to macOS CoreAnimation/Metal output backpressure: ApplyBackpressure lasts 129.025 ms inside ImageTransportSurfaceOverlayMac::Present / SwapBuffers at +305.907 ms, with only 1.489 ms thread CPU. Nested ApplyBackpressure::Metal intervals account for essentially all the wall time. This is not a 100 ms main-thread UI paint or application JavaScript task.",
    "ANGLE WaitForCommandsToBeScheduled immediately before the stalled Present is only 0.008 ms. This argues against that specific app-ANGLE scheduling wait being the direct long section; it does not exclude earlier app GPU work contributing to outstanding Metal work.",
    "The hidden-UI capture also has CoreAnimation/Metal backpressure (largest early occurrence roughly 42.6 ms). Therefore the phenomenon is not exclusively created by visible UI and absence of >25 ms RAF samples does not mean no downstream waits occurred.",
    "Visible UI adds main-thread paint and compositor raster for #player (first 500 ms Paint 1.376 ms vs hidden UI 0.116 ms, RasterTask 4 vs 0), but recorded GPU RendererRasterWorker aggregate is actually LOWER in full UI (118.289 ms vs 169.476 ms). The traces use different input methods and have different WebGL service-call counts (73 vs 330 in the first 500 ms), so these windows are not equivalent work samples. The hidden-UI intervention is suggestive but does not establish UI as the owner of the Metal backlog or identify a CSS shader/texture upload.",
    "No explicitly named shader compilation/upload event is recorded in either capture. Deep trace gives a much narrower wait location but still no named shader compile duration or resource ID tying the wait to a specific app/UI pipeline. It is not justified to conclude Skia shader compilation or app path tessellation from this data.",
    "Retain the measured cold stall. Do not improve the reported score by hiding UI or omitting the first seconds. Any rendering change should be tested with the complete UI and click-driven replay again."
  ],
  "recommendations": [
    "Use the hidden-UI intervention only to choose an investigation direction, not to claim UI causality. Match the real input method, input-relative time window and actual app/render work counts before comparing UI raster/compositing contributions. Current deep captures locate the wait but their early workload mix differs.",
    "If pursuing exact attribution, inspect Metal command-buffer/fence ownership or native GPU signposts for the outstanding work waited by CALayerTreeCoordinator, and annotate app image/atlas uploads separately. Current host trace lacks that ownership.",
    "Keep application AA/path logic changes separate from UI/compositor experiments; this trace does not implicate path geometry CPU or the brief ANGLE schedule wait.",
